WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.362
The kit's CMDB inventory script turns
each owner into a group and a tag.

2
00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:10.464
A config owner of arc machine configuration,
Ansible, Intune and so on becomes a

3
00:00:10.464 --> 00:00:15.487
group, and a Config Owner tag in the
spelling of this workbook's Arc track guide.

4
00:00:15.487 --> 00:00:17.450
The patch owner works the same way.

5
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.757
An empty owner on a tier one or
two record goes to a missing group,

6
00:00:21.757 --> 00:00:25.649
which is a finding under NIST
special publication eight

7
00:00:25.649 --> 00:00:29.625
hundred fifty-three control
CM-8, inventory, never a target.

8
00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:31.575
And the wave becomes a wave group.

9
00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:33.375
Three rules fail closed.

10
00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:36.163
An owner value the script
doesn't know stops it.

11
00:00:36.163 --> 00:00:40.272
A typo like update manager written
as one word would otherwise create a

12
00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:44.450
group nothing reads, and drop the server
out of the group that protects it.

13
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.500
A wave that isn't a number stops it.

14
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.778
And a server is a candidate only if its
record has both owners, a resource group,

15
00:00:51.778 --> 00:00:54.582
a region, a wave, and
tag values Arc accepts;

16
00:00:54.582 --> 00:01:00.025
Learn's azcmagent connect reference takes
the tags as one comma-delimited list.

17
00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:02.950
Otherwise it's blocked,
with the reasons recorded.

18
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.920
All three are the kit's design.
